1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,239
PART 2 chapter 1.

2
00:00:04,239 --> 00:00:08,760
It was the middle of the morning, and Winston had left the cubicle to go to the lavatory.

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:14,859
A solitary figure was coming towards him from the other end of the long brightly lit corridor.

4
00:00:14,859 --> 00:00:17,318
It was the girl with dark hair.

5
00:00:17,318 --> 00:00:22,540
Four days had gone past since the evening when he had run into her outside the junk shop

6
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:27,279
As she came nearer he saw that her right arm was in a sling, not noticeable at a

7
00:00:27,279 --> 00:00:30,760
distance because it was of the same color as her overalls.

8
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:33,700
Probably she had crushed her hand while swinging around one of the big

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,299
kaleidoscopes on which the plots of novels were 'roughed in'.

10
00:00:37,299 --> 00:00:42,879
It was a common accident in the Fiction Department there were perhaps four meters apart

11
00:00:42,879 --> 00:00:45,939
when the girl stumbled and fell almost flat on her face.

12
00:00:45,939 --> 00:00:48,700
A sharp cry of pain was rung out of her.

13
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,340
She must have fallen right on the injured arm.

14
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,139
Winston stopped short.

15
00:00:53,139 --> 00:00:57,398
The girl had risen to her knees. Her face had turned a milky yellow color against

16
00:00:57,398 --> 00:01:00,219
which her mouth stood out redder than ever.

17
00:01:00,219 --> 00:01:06,398
Her eyes were fixed on his, with an appealing expression that looked more like fear than pain.

18
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:11,200
A curious emotion stirred in Winston's heart. In front of him was an enemy who

19
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:15,639
was trying to kill him: in front of him, also, was a human creature, in pain and

20
00:01:15,639 --> 00:01:17,920
perhaps with a broken bone.

21
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,398
Already he had instinctively started forward to help her.

22
00:01:21,398 --> 00:01:25,359
In the moment when he had seen her fall on the bandage at arm, it had been as

23
00:01:25,359 --> 00:01:28,059
though he felt the pain in his own body.

24
00:01:28,059 --> 00:01:29,920
'You're hurt?' he said.

25
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:35,680
'It's nothing. My arm. It'll be all right in a second he spoke as though her heart were

26
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:39,398
fluttering. She had certainly turned very pale.

27
00:01:39,398 --> 00:01:41,559
'You haven't broken anything?'

28
00:01:41,559 --> 00:01:45,159
'No, I'm all right. It hurt for a moment, that's all.'

29
00:01:45,159 --> 00:01:48,520
She held out her free hand to him, and he helped her up.

30
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:52,659
She had regained some of her color and appeared very much better.

31
00:01:52,659 --> 00:01:59,620
'It's nothing,' she repeated shortly. 'I only gave my wrist a bit of a bang s comrade!'

32
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:03,040
And with that she walked on in the direction in which she had been going, as

33
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,859
briskly as though it had really been nothing.

34
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:09,100
The whole incident could not have taken as much as half a minute.

35
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:13,120
Not to let one's feelings appear in one's face was a habit that had acquired

36
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:16,898
the status of an instinct, and in any case they had been standing straight in

37
00:02:16,898 --> 00:02:19,359
front of a telescreen when the thing happened.

38
00:02:19,359 --> 00:02:23,620
Nevertheless it had been very difficult not to betray a momentary surprise, for

39
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:26,680
in the two or three seconds while he was helping her up the girl had slipped

40
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:28,659
something into his hand.

41
00:02:28,659 --> 00:02:31,840
There was no question that she had done it intentionally.

42
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:34,478
It was something small and flat.

43
00:02:34,478 --> 00:02:38,200
As he passed through the lavatory door he transferred it to his pocket and felt

44
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:40,420
it with the tips of his fingers.

45
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:44,559
It was a scrap of paper folded into a square.

46
00:02:44,559 --> 00:02:48,219
While he stood at the urinal he managed, with a little more fingering, to get it

47
00:02:48,219 --> 00:02:52,898
unfolded. Obviously there must be a message of some kind written on it.

48
00:02:52,898 --> 00:02:56,020
For a moment he was tempted to take it into one of the water closets and read

49
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:59,920
it at once. But that would be shocking folly, as he well knew.

50
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:03,159
There was no place where you could be more certain that the telescreens were

51
00:03:03,159 --> 00:03:05,799
watched continuously.

52
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:10,180
He went back to his cubicle, sat down through the fragment of paper casually

53
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:14,079
among the other papers on the desk, put on his spectacles and hitched the speak

54
00:03:14,079 --> 00:03:16,180
right towards him.

55
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:20,079
'Five minutes,' he told himself, 'five minutes at the very least!'

56
00:03:20,079 --> 00:03:23,559
His heart bumped in his breast with frightening loudness.

57
00:03:23,559 --> 00:03:27,219
Fortunately the piece of work he was engaged on was mere routine, the

58
00:03:27,219 --> 00:03:31,840
rectification of a long list of figures, not needing close attention.

59
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:36,398
Whatever was written on the paper, it must have some kind of political meaning.

60
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:40,420
So far as he could see there were two possibilities. One, much the more likely,

61
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:44,379
was that the girl was an agent of the Thought Police, just as he had feared.

62
00:03:44,379 --> 00:03:47,559
He did not know why the Thought Police should choose to deliver their messages

63
00:03:47,559 --> 00:03:51,279
in such a fashion, but perhaps they had their reasons.

64
00:03:51,279 --> 00:03:55,299
The thing that was written on the paper might be a threat, a summons, an order to

65
00:03:55,299 --> 00:03:58,719
commit suicide, a trap of some description.

66
00:03:58,719 --> 00:04:02,260
But there was another, wilder possibility that kept raising its head, though he

67
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:04,180
tried vainly to suppress it.

68
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:08,079
This was, that the message did not come from the Thought Police at all,

69
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:14,200
but from some kind of underground organization Perhaps the Brotherhood existed after all!

70
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:16,478
Perhaps the girl was part of it!

71
00:04:16,478 --> 00:04:20,620
No doubt the idea was absurd, but it had sprung into his mind in the very instant

72
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:23,318
of feeling the scrap of paper in his hand.

73
00:04:23,318 --> 00:04:26,559
It was not till a couple of minutes later that the other, more probable

74
00:04:26,559 --> 00:04:29,020
explanation had occurred to him.

75
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:33,398
And even now, though his intellect told him that the message probably meant death—

76
00:04:33,398 --> 00:04:37,779
—still, that was not what he believed, and the unreasonable hope persisted, and his

77
00:04:37,779 --> 00:04:41,859
heart banged, and it was with difficulty that he kept his voice from trembling as

78
00:04:41,859 --> 00:04:47,500
he murmured his figures into the speak right He rolled up the completed bundle of

79
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:49,840
work and slid it into the pneumatic tube.

80
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:55,840
Eight minutes had gone by. He readjusted his spectacles on his nose side and drew

81
00:04:55,840 --> 00:05:00,159
the next batch of work towards him, with a scrap of paper on top of it. He

82
00:05:00,159 --> 00:05:07,719
flattened it out. On it was written, in a large unformed handwriting: I love you.

83
00:05:07,719 --> 00:05:10,959
For several seconds he was too stunned even to throw the incriminating thing

84
00:05:10,959 --> 00:05:12,520
into the memory hole.

85
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:15,760
When he did so, although he knew very well the danger of showing too much

86
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:19,779
interest, he could not resist reading it once again, just to make sure that the

87
00:05:19,779 --> 00:05:22,680
words were really there.

88
00:05:23,318 --> 00:05:26,559
For the rest of the morning it was very difficult to work.

89
00:05:26,559 --> 00:05:30,279
What was even worse than having to focus his mind on a series of niggling jobs

90
00:05:30,279 --> 00:05:34,180
was the need to conceal his agitation from the telescreen.

91
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:37,180
He felt as though a fire were burning in his belly.

92
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:41,200
Lunch in the hot, crowded, noise-filled canteen was torment.

93
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:45,100
He had hoped to be alone for a little while during the lunch hour, but as bad

94
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:49,359
luck would have it the imbecile Parsons flopped down beside him, the tang of his

95
00:05:49,359 --> 00:05:53,379
sweat almost defeating the tinny smell of stew, and kept up a stream of talk

96
00:05:53,379 --> 00:05:55,898
about the preparations for Hate Week.

97
00:05:55,898 --> 00:05:59,799
He was particularly enthusiastic about a papier-mache model of Big Brother's head,

98
00:05:59,799 --> 00:06:05,139
two meters wide, which was being made for the occasion by his daughter's troop of Spies.

99
00:06:05,139 --> 00:06:08,859
The irritating thing was that in the racket of voices Winston could hardly

100
00:06:08,859 --> 00:06:12,459
hear what Parsons was saying, and was constantly having to ask for some

101
00:06:12,459 --> 00:06:14,920
fatuous remark to be repeated.

102
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,340
Just once he caught a glimpse of the girl, at a table with two other girls at

103
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:20,079
the far end of the room.

104
00:06:20,079 --> 00:06:25,680
She appeared not to have seen him, and he did not look in that direction again.

105
00:06:26,318 --> 00:06:28,659
The afternoon was more bearable.

106
00:06:28,659 --> 00:06:32,799
Immediately after lunch there arrived a delicate, difficult piece of work which

107
00:06:32,799 --> 00:06:36,939
would take several hours and necessitated putting everything else aside.

108
00:06:36,939 --> 00:06:41,620
It consisted in falsifying a series of production reports of two years ago, in

109
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:45,398
such a way as to cast discredit on a prominent member of the Inner Party who

110
00:06:45,398 --> 00:06:47,139
was now under a cloud.

111
00:06:47,139 --> 00:06:50,978
This was the kind of thing that Winston was good at, and for more than two hours

112
00:06:50,978 --> 00:06:54,700
he succeeded in shutting the girl out of his mind altogether.

113
00:06:54,700 --> 00:07:00,639
Then the memory of her face came back, and with it a raging, intolerable desire to be alone.

114
00:07:00,639 --> 00:07:04,898
Until he could be alone it was impossible to think this new development out.

115
00:07:04,898 --> 00:07:10,420
Tonight was one of his Knights at the Community center He wolfed another tasteless meal in the

116
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:14,318
canteen, hurried off to the center took part in the solemn foolery of a

117
00:07:14,318 --> 00:07:18,579
'discussion group', played two games of table tennis, swallowed several glasses

118
00:07:18,579 --> 00:07:23,978
of gin and sat for half an hour through a lecture entitled yingsok in relation to chess'.

119
00:07:23,978 --> 00:07:28,840
His soul writhed with boredom, but for once he had had no impulse to shirk his

120
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:32,799
evening at the center At the sight of the words I love you the

121
00:07:32,799 --> 00:07:38,859
desire to stay alive had welled up in him, and the taking of minor risks suddenly seemed stupid.

122
00:07:38,859 --> 00:07:43,299
It was not till 23 hours, when he was home and in bed— —in the darkness, where

123
00:07:43,299 --> 00:07:46,840
you were safe even from the telescreen so long as you kept silent— —that he was

124
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:50,100
able to think continuously.

125
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:53,978
It was a physical problem that had to be solved: how to get in touch with the girl

126
00:07:53,978 --> 00:07:55,659
and arrange a meeting.

127
00:07:55,659 --> 00:07:59,079
He did not consider any longer the possibility that she might be laying

128
00:07:59,079 --> 00:08:00,879
some kind of trap for him.

129
00:08:00,879 --> 00:08:06,059
He knew that it was not so, because of her unmistakable agitation when she handed him the note.

130
00:08:06,059 --> 00:08:10,779
Obviously she had been frightened out of her wits, as well she might be. Nor did

131
00:08:10,779 --> 00:08:13,959
the idea of refusing her advances even crossed his mind.

132
00:08:13,959 --> 00:08:17,859
Only five nights ago he had contemplated smashing her skull in with a cobblestone;

133
00:08:17,859 --> 00:08:20,200
but that was of no importance.

134
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:24,879
He thought of her naked, youthful body, as he had seen it in his dream.

135
00:08:24,879 --> 00:08:29,200
He had imagined her a fool like all the rest of them, her head stuffed with lies

136
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:32,139
and hatred a belly full of ice.

137
00:08:32,139 --> 00:08:35,680
A kind of fever seized him at the thought that he might lose her, the white

138
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:38,559
youthful body might slip away from him!

139
00:08:38,559 --> 00:08:41,739
What he feared more than anything else was that she would simply change her

140
00:08:41,739 --> 00:08:46,120
mind if he did not get in touch with her quickly the physical difficulty of meeting was

141
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:47,379
enormous.

142
00:08:47,379 --> 00:08:51,398
It was like trying to make a move at chess when you were already mated.

143
00:08:51,398 --> 00:08:54,760
Whichever way you turned, the telescreen faced you.

144
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:57,879
Actually, all the possible ways of communicating with her had occurred to

145
00:08:57,879 --> 00:09:01,840
him within five minutes of reading the note; but now, with time to think, he went

146
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:07,299
over them one by one, as though laying out a row of instruments on the table.

147
00:09:07,299 --> 00:09:11,559
Obviously the kind of encounter that had happened this morning could not be repeated.

148
00:09:11,559 --> 00:09:14,439
If she had worked in the Records Department it might have been

149
00:09:14,439 --> 00:09:18,760
comparatively simple, but he had only a very dim idea of whereabouts in the

150
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:23,200
building the Fiction Department lay, and he had no pretty text for going there. If

151
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:26,799
he had known where she lived, and at what time she left work, he could have

152
00:09:26,799 --> 00:09:30,939
contrived to meet her somewhere on her way home; but to try to follow her and

153
00:09:30,939 --> 00:09:34,779
was not safe, because it would mean loitering about outside the Ministry,

154
00:09:34,779 --> 00:09:37,239
which was bound to be noticed.

155
00:09:37,239 --> 00:09:40,779
As for sending a letter through the mails, it was out of the question.

156
00:09:40,779 --> 00:09:45,159
By her routine that was not even secret, all letters were opened in transit.

157
00:09:45,159 --> 00:09:47,680
Actually, few people ever wrote letters.

158
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:51,279
For the messages that it was occasionally necessary to send, there

159
00:09:51,279 --> 00:09:54,939
were printed postcards with long lists of phrases, and you struck out the ones

160
00:09:54,939 --> 00:09:56,920
that were inapplicable.

161
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:00,939
In any case he did not know the girl's name, let alone her address.

162
00:10:00,939 --> 00:10:04,840
Finally he decided that the safest place was the canteen.

163
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:08,079
If he could get her at a table by herself, somewhere in the middle of the

164
00:10:08,079 --> 00:10:13,719
room, not too near the telescreens, and with the sufficient buzz of conversation all round—

165
00:10:13,719 --> 00:10:20,340
—if these conditions endured for, say 30 seconds, it might be possible to exchange a few words.

166
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:25,600
For a week after this, life was like a restless dream. On the next day she did

167
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,920
not appear in the canteen until he was leaving it, the whistle having already blown.

168
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:33,520
Presumably she had been changed on to a later shift.

169
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:36,159
They passed each other without a glance.

170
00:10:36,159 --> 00:10:40,120
On the day after that she was in the canteen at the usual time, but with three

171
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:43,299
other girls and immediately under a telescreen.

172
00:10:43,299 --> 00:10:46,959
Then for three dreadful days she did not appear at all.

173
00:10:46,959 --> 00:10:50,859
His whole mind and body seemed to be afflicted with an unbearable sensitivity,

174
00:10:50,859 --> 00:10:55,959
a sort of transparency, which made every movement, every sound, every contact, every

175
00:10:55,959 --> 00:10:59,279
word that he had to speak or listen to and agony.

176
00:10:59,279 --> 00:11:03,700
Even in sleep he could not altogether escape from her image. He did not touch

177
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:05,559
the diary during those days.

178
00:11:05,559 --> 00:11:09,040
If there was any relief, it was in his work, in which he could sometimes forget

179
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:11,680
himself for 10 minutes at a stretch.

180
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:15,100
He had absolutely no clue as to what had happened to her.

181
00:11:15,100 --> 00:11:17,439
There was no inquiry he could make.

182
00:11:17,439 --> 00:11:21,459
She might have been vaporized, she might have committed suicide, she might have

183
00:11:21,459 --> 00:11:26,139
been transferred to the other end of Oceania: worst and likeliest of all, she might

184
00:11:26,139 --> 00:11:30,000
simply have changed her mind and decided to avoid him.

185
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,898
The next day she reappeared. Her arm was out of the sling and she had a band of

186
00:11:34,898 --> 00:11:36,760
sticking plaster around her wrist.

187
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:40,238
The relief of seeing her was so great that he could not resist staring

188
00:11:40,238 --> 00:11:42,879
directly at her for several seconds.

189
00:11:42,879 --> 00:11:46,478
On the following day he very nearly succeeded in speaking to her.

190
00:11:46,478 --> 00:11:49,659
When he came into the canteen she was sitting at a table well out from the

191
00:11:49,659 --> 00:11:51,520
wall, and was quite alone.

192
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:54,219
It was early, and the place was not very full.

193
00:11:54,219 --> 00:11:58,299
The queue edged forward till Winston was almost at the Carter then was held up

194
00:11:58,299 --> 00:12:01,478
for two minutes because someone in front was complaining that he had not received

195
00:12:01,478 --> 00:12:07,059
his tablet of saccharin But the girl was still alone when Winston secured his tray and began to

196
00:12:07,059 --> 00:12:08,680
make for her table.

197
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:13,779
He walked casually towards her, his eyes searching for a place at some table beyond her.

198
00:12:13,779 --> 00:12:18,639
She was perhaps three meters away from him. Another two seconds would do it.

199
00:12:18,639 --> 00:12:24,520
Then a voice behind him called, 'Smith!' He pretended not to hear. 'Smith!' repeated the

200
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:26,020
voice, more loudly.

201
00:12:26,020 --> 00:12:30,459
It was no use. He turned round. A blonde-headed silly-faced young man

202
00:12:30,459 --> 00:12:35,020
named Wiltshire whom he barely knew, was inviting him with a smile to a vacant

203
00:12:35,020 --> 00:12:36,879
place at his table.

204
00:12:36,879 --> 00:12:39,279
It was not safe to refuse.

205
00:12:39,279 --> 00:12:44,020
After having been recognized he could not go and sit at a table with an unattended girl.

206
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:46,059
It was too noticeable.

207
00:12:46,059 --> 00:12:50,340
He sat down with a friendly smile a silly blonde face beamed into his.

208
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:55,600
Winston had a hallucination of himself smashing a pickaxe right into the middle of it.

209
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:59,318
The girls table filled up a few minutes later.

210
00:12:59,318 --> 00:13:03,398
But she must have seen him coming towards her, and perhaps she would take the hint.

211
00:13:03,398 --> 00:13:08,559
Next day he took care to arrive early surely enough, she was at the table in

212
00:13:08,559 --> 00:13:11,200
about the same place, and again alone.

213
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:15,040
The person immediately ahead of him in the queue was a small swiftly moving

214
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:19,120
beetle-like man with a flat face and tiny, suspicious eyes.

215
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:22,779
As Winston turned away from the counter with his tray, he saw that the little man

216
00:13:22,779 --> 00:13:25,478
was making straight for the girls table.

217
00:13:25,478 --> 00:13:29,978
His hopes sank again. There was a vacant place at a table further away, but

218
00:13:29,978 --> 00:13:32,859
something in the little man's appearance suggested that he would be sufficiently

219
00:13:32,859 --> 00:13:37,180
attentive to his own comfort to choose the emptiest table.

220
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:39,760
With eyes at his heart Winston followed.

221
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:42,939
It was no use unless he could get the girl alone.

222
00:13:42,939 --> 00:13:46,000
At this moment there was a tremendous crash.

223
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:49,898
The little man was sprawling on all fours, his tray had gone flying, two

224
00:13:49,898 --> 00:13:53,318
streams of soup and coffee were flowing across the floor.

225
00:13:53,318 --> 00:13:57,040
He started to his feet with a malignant glance at Winston, whom he evidently

226
00:13:57,040 --> 00:13:59,139
suspected of having tripped him up.

227
00:13:59,139 --> 00:14:03,520
But it was all right. Five seconds later, with the thundering heart, Winston was

228
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:06,459
sitting at the girl's table.

229
00:14:06,459 --> 00:14:11,379
He did not look at her. He unpacked his tray and promptly began eating.

230
00:14:11,379 --> 00:14:15,879
It was all important to speak at once, before anyone else came, but now a

231
00:14:15,879 --> 00:14:20,260
terrible fear had taken possession of him. A week had gone by since she had

232
00:14:20,260 --> 00:14:24,879
first approached him. She would have changed her mind, she must have changed her mind!

233
00:14:24,879 --> 00:14:28,779
It was impossible that this affair should end successfully; such things did

234
00:14:28,779 --> 00:14:30,939
not happen in real life.

235
00:14:30,939 --> 00:14:34,180
He might have flinched altogether from speaking if at this moment he had not

236
00:14:34,180 --> 00:14:38,379
seen Ampleforth, the hairy eared poet, wandering limply round the room with a

237
00:14:38,379 --> 00:14:41,020
tray, looking for a place to sit down.

238
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:45,100
In his vague way Ampleforth was attached to Winston, and would certainly sit down

239
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:47,799
at his table if he caught sight of him.

240
00:14:47,799 --> 00:14:51,639
There was perhaps a minute in which to act. Both Winston and the girl were

241
00:14:51,639 --> 00:14:57,520
eating steadily. The stuff they were eating was a thin stew, actually a soup, of haricot beans.

242
00:14:57,520 --> 00:15:00,459
In a low murmur Winston began speaking.

243
00:15:00,459 --> 00:15:04,359
Neither of them looked up; steadily they spooned the watery stuff

244
00:15:04,359 --> 00:15:08,559
into their mouths, and between spoonfuls exchanged the few necessary words in low

245
00:15:08,559 --> 00:15:11,318
expressionless voices.

246
00:15:11,318 --> 00:15:16,779
'What time do you leave work 1830 Where can we meet?'

247
00:15:16,779 --> 00:15:19,420
'Victory Square, near the monument.'

248
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:21,579
'It's full of telescreens.'

249
00:15:21,579 --> 00:15:25,420
'It doesn't matter if there's a crowd.' 'Any signal?'

250
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:28,120
'No. Don't come up to me until you see me

251
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:32,738
among a lot of people. And don't look at me. Just keep somewhere near me.'

252
00:15:32,738 --> 00:15:35,738
'What time 19 hours.'

253
00:15:35,738 --> 00:15:42,459
'All right' Ampleforth failed to see Winston and sat down at another table.

254
00:15:42,459 --> 00:15:45,879
They did not speak again, and, so far as it was possible for two people sitting

255
00:15:45,879 --> 00:15:49,840
on opposite sides of the same table, they did not look at one another.

256
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:56,398
The girl finished her lunch quickly and made off, while Winston stated to smoke a cigarette.

257
00:15:56,639 --> 00:16:01,299
Winston was in Victory Square before the appointed time. He wandered round the

258
00:16:01,299 --> 00:16:04,840
base of the enormous fluted column, at the top of which Big Brother's statue

259
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:08,859
gazed southward towards the skies where he had vanquished the Eurasian airplanes

260
00:16:08,859 --> 00:16:14,799
the East Asian airplanes it had been, a few years ago) in the Battle of Airstrip One.

261
00:16:14,799 --> 00:16:18,459
In the street in front of it there was a statue of a man on horseback which was

262
00:16:18,459 --> 00:16:20,978
supposed to represent Oliver Cromwell.

263
00:16:20,978 --> 00:16:24,760
At five minutes past the hour the girl had still not appeared.

264
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:30,100
Again the terrible fear seized upon Winston. She was not coming, she had changed her mind!

265
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:33,879
He walked slowly up to the north side of the square and got a sort of pale

266
00:16:33,879 --> 00:16:38,139
colored pleasure from identifying Saint Martin's church, whose bells, when it had

267
00:16:38,139 --> 00:16:42,520
bells, had chimed 'You owe me three farthings.'

268
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:45,520
Then he saw the girl standing at the base of the monument, reading or

269
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:49,238
pretending to read a poster which ranspiringly up the column.

270
00:16:49,238 --> 00:16:53,079
It was not safe to go near her until some more people had accumulated.

271
00:16:53,079 --> 00:16:55,959
There were telescreens all around the pediment.

272
00:16:55,959 --> 00:16:59,500
But at this moment there was a din of shouting and a zoom of heavy vehicles

273
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:01,059
from somewhere to the left.

274
00:17:01,059 --> 00:17:05,559
Suddenly everyone seemed to be running across the square. The girl nipped nimbly

275
00:17:05,559 --> 00:17:08,920
round the lions at the base of the monument and joined in the rush.

276
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:13,659
Winston followed. As he ran, he gathered from some shouted remarks that a convoy

277
00:17:13,659 --> 00:17:17,020
of Eurasian prisoners was passing.

278
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:20,978
Already a dense mass of people was blocking the south side of the square.

279
00:17:20,978 --> 00:17:24,579
Winston, at normal times the kind of person who gravitates to the outer edge

280
00:17:24,579 --> 00:17:30,579
of any kind of scrimmage, shoved buttered squirmed his way forward into the heart of the crowd.

281
00:17:30,579 --> 00:17:34,238
Soon he was within arm's length of the girl, but the way was blocked by an

282
00:17:34,238 --> 00:17:38,559
enormous Pro with an almost equally enormous woman, presumably his wife, who

283
00:17:38,559 --> 00:17:41,920
seemed to form an impenetrable wall of flesh.

284
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:46,180
Winston wriggled himself sideways, and with a violent lunge managed to drive

285
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:48,340
his shoulder between them.

286
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:51,398
For a moment it felt as though his entrails were being ground to pulp

287
00:17:51,398 --> 00:17:55,959
between the two muscular hips, then he had broken through, sweating a little.

288
00:17:55,959 --> 00:18:01,840
He was next to the girl. They were shoulder to shoulder, both staring fixedly in front of them.

289
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:06,100
A long line of trucks, with wooden-faced guards armed with submachine guns

290
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:09,939
standing upright in each corner, was passing slowly down the street.

291
00:18:09,939 --> 00:18:13,779
In the trucks little yellow men in shabby greenish uniforms were squatting,

292
00:18:13,779 --> 00:18:15,579
jammed close together.

293
00:18:15,579 --> 00:18:21,159
Their sad Mongolian faces gazed out over the sides of the trucks, utterly and curious

294
00:18:21,159 --> 00:18:24,760
Occasionally when a truck jolted there was a Clank Clank of metal:

295
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:29,318
all the prisoners were wearing leg irons truckload after truckload of the sad

296
00:18:29,318 --> 00:18:34,238
face has passed. Winston knew they were there, but he saw them only intermittently.

297
00:18:34,238 --> 00:18:38,978
The girl's shoulder, and her arm right down to the elbow, were pressed against his.

298
00:18:38,978 --> 00:18:42,459
Her cheek was almost near enough for him to feel its warmth.

299
00:18:42,459 --> 00:18:46,959
She had immediately taken charge of the situation, just as she had done in the canteen.

300
00:18:46,959 --> 00:18:51,219
She began speaking in the same expressionless voice as before, with lips

301
00:18:51,219 --> 00:18:56,799
barely moving, a mere murmur easily drowned by the dinner voices and the rumbling of the trucks.

302
00:18:56,799 --> 00:18:58,359
'Can you hear me?'

303
00:18:58,359 --> 00:19:01,898
'Yes.' 'Can you get Sunday afternoon off?'

304
00:19:01,898 --> 00:19:03,159
'Yes.'

305
00:19:03,159 --> 00:19:06,340
'Then listen carefully. You'll have to remember this.

306
00:19:06,340 --> 00:19:10,600
Go to Paddington Station— With a sort of military precision that

307
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:13,898
astonished him, she outlined the route that he was to follow.

308
00:19:13,898 --> 00:19:18,700
A half hour railway journey; turn left outside the station; two kilometers along

309
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:24,040
the road; a gate with the top bar missing; a path across a field; a grass-grown lane;

310
00:19:24,040 --> 00:19:28,059
a track between bushes; a dead tree with moss on it.

311
00:19:28,059 --> 00:19:30,879
It was as though she had a map inside her head.

312
00:19:30,879 --> 00:19:34,120
'Can you remember all that?' she murmured finally.

313
00:19:34,120 --> 00:19:35,500
'Yes.'

314
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:37,779
'You turn left, then right, then left again.

315
00:19:37,779 --> 00:19:39,818
And the gate's got no top bar.'

316
00:19:39,818 --> 00:19:42,100
'Yes. What time?'

317
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:47,379
'About 15 You may have to wait. I'll get there by another way.

318
00:19:47,379 --> 00:19:49,779
Are you sure you remember everything?'

319
00:19:49,779 --> 00:19:51,159
'Yes.'

320
00:19:51,159 --> 00:19:54,279
'Then get away from me as quick as you can.'

321
00:19:54,279 --> 00:19:56,379
She need not have told him that.

322
00:19:56,379 --> 00:19:59,920
But for the moment they could not extricate themselves from the crowd.

323
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:04,600
The trucks were still filing past, the people still insatiably gaping.

324
00:20:04,600 --> 00:20:08,439
At the start there had been a few booze and hisses, but it came only from the

325
00:20:08,439 --> 00:20:11,318
Party members among the crowd, and had soon stopped.

326
00:20:11,318 --> 00:20:14,439
The prevailing emotion was simply curiosity.

327
00:20:14,439 --> 00:20:19,299
Foreigners, whether from Eurasia or from East Asia were a kind of strange animal.

328
00:20:19,299 --> 00:20:23,139
One literally never saw them except in the guise of prisoners, and even as

329
00:20:23,139 --> 00:20:26,200
prisoners one never got more than a momentary glimpse of them.

330
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:30,100
Nor did one know what became of them, apart from the few who were hanged as

331
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:34,959
war criminals the others simply vanished, presumably into forced labor camps.

332
00:20:34,959 --> 00:20:39,879
The round Mongol faces had given way to faces of a more European type, dirty,

333
00:20:39,879 --> 00:20:44,859
bearded and exhausted. From over scrubby cheekbones eyes looked into Winston's,

334
00:20:44,859 --> 00:20:49,238
sometimes with strange intensity, and flashed away again.

335
00:20:49,238 --> 00:20:54,279
The convoy was drawing to an end. In the last truck he could see an aged man, his

336
00:20:54,279 --> 00:20:58,120
face a massive grizzled hair, standing upright with wrists crossed in front of

337
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:01,478
him, as though he were used to having them bound together.

338
00:21:01,478 --> 00:21:05,620
It was almost time for Winston and the girl to part. But at the last moment,

339
00:21:05,620 --> 00:21:11,978
while the crowd still hemmed them in, her hand felt for his and gave it a fleeting squeeze.

340
00:21:11,978 --> 00:21:15,700
It could not have been 10 seconds, and yet it seemed a long time that their

341
00:21:15,700 --> 00:21:17,738
hands were clasped together.

342
00:21:17,738 --> 00:21:20,920
He had time to learn every detail of her hand.

343
00:21:20,920 --> 00:21:25,478
He explored the long fingers, the shapely nails, the work hard and palm with its

344
00:21:25,478 --> 00:21:29,020
row of calluses the smooth flesh under the wrist.

345
00:21:29,020 --> 00:21:32,559
Merely from feeling it he would have known it by sight.

346
00:21:32,559 --> 00:21:35,620
In the same instant it occurred to him that he did not know what color the

347
00:21:35,620 --> 00:21:37,000
girl's eyes were.

348
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:41,439
They were probably brown, but people with dark hair sometimes had blue eyes.

349
00:21:41,439 --> 00:21:45,639
To turn his head and look at her would have been inconceivable folly.

350
00:21:45,639 --> 00:21:49,959
With hands locked together, invisible among the press of bodies, they stared

351
00:21:49,959 --> 00:21:53,799
steadily in front of them, and instead of the eyes of the girl, the eyes of the

352
00:21:53,799 --> 00:21:58,000
aged prisoner gazed mournfully at Winston out of nests of hair
